-### Parameters
-*   `quad_split`: Boolean. `True` outputs triangles via Delaunay splitting; `False` outputs pure quads [S1, unofficial].
-*   `pad`: Integer. Set to 1 for sparse voxel grids to ensure all 4 incident voxels surrounding sign-crossing edges are in GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
-
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Coplanar Normals**: If normals are nearly coplanar, the QEF matrix becomes ill-conditioned [S1, unofficial]. The solver uses Regularized SVD Truncation (clamping singular values $< 0.01 \sigma_{\max}$) and mass-centroid regularization to prevent the vertex from drifting outside the voxel [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Sharp Features**: Trilinear gradient smoothing across $C^0$ slope discontinuities can blur sharp edges [S1, unofficial]. Passing exact Hermite or triangle normals via `grid_normals` is required to preserve razor-sharp knife edges on CAD models [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Single Vertex Limitation**: Each voxel contains at most one dual vertex [S1, unofficial]. Complex sub-voxel topology with multiple disconnected sheets inside a single voxel can lead to non-manifold geometry (pinch points) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Brute-Force Fallback**: Some 3D implementations disable QEF solving entirely in favor of brute-force search, but this is less efficient and not the standard approach for high-performance variants [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Single Dual Vertex Limitation:** Each voxel contains at most one vertex. Complex sub-voxel topology with multiple disconnected sheets inside a single cell can result in shared vertices (pinch points) and non-manifold geometry [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Normal Dependence:** Trilinear gradient smoothing across $C^0$ slope discontinuities blurs sharp edges. Exact Hermite or triangle normals must be provided via `grid_normals` [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Padding:** Sparse voxel grids require `pad=1` to ensure all 4 incident voxels surrounding a sign-crossing edge are present in memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Coplanar Normals:** When normals are nearly coplanar, the QEF system becomes ill-conditioned. Regularized SVD truncation is required to prevent the vertex from drifting outside the voxel boundary [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Quadric Error Function (QEF) for Sharp Features](1604-quadric-error-function-qef-for-sharp-features.md)
-This method computes the optimal vertex position for sharp features (corners and edges) in dual contouring by minimizing the distance to multiple intersecting planes defined by sample points and their normals. It distinguishes itself from standard dual contouring by explicitly handling underdetermined systems (sharp edges) via least-squares minimization, ensuring the resulting vertex lies equidistant from all contributing planes.
+A vertex relocation technique that positions a single vertex within an octree cell to accurately represent sharp features (edges and corners). It sets itself apart from siblings like [Brute-Force QEF Solver](4403-brute-force-qef-solver.md) by explicitly constructing "supporting planes" in addition to tangent planes to stabilize minimization and prevent numerical instabilities. Choose this when standard QEM approaches fail to preserve high-curvature regions. Link: [Quadric Error Function (QEF) for Sharp Features](1604-quadric-error-function-qef-for-sharp-features.md).
 
 ### [QEF Data Accumulation](3712-qef-data-accumulation.md)
-QEF Data Accumulation is a data structure and incremental update mechanism for aggregating the components of a Quadratic Error Function (QEF) from multiple plane constraints. It solves the problem of efficiently storing and combining linear constraints (points and normals) without retaining the raw input data, allowing the downstream [Dual Contouring QEF Solver](1424-dual-contouring-qef-solver.md) to reconstruct the optimal vertex position from the accumulated normal equations. It differs from storing individual points by maintaining only the pre-computed sums required for the least-squares solution, and it supports merging accumulators from different sources (e.g., multiple grid cells or features) via element-wise addition.
+A data structure and incremental update mechanism for aggregating QEF components from multiple plane constraints without retaining raw input data. It differs from storing individual points by maintaining only pre-computed sums required for the least-squares solution and supports merging accumulators via element-wise addition. Choose this for efficient GPU-based aggregation. Link: [QEF Data Accumulation](3712-qef-data-accumulation.md).
 
 ### [Occupancy-Based Dual Contouring](3799-occupancy-based-dual-contouring.md)
-Occupancy-Based Dual Contouring (ODC) is a dual contouring method designed to extract meshes from neural implicit functions defined in continuous 3D space, such as Occupancy Networks, IM-NET, or DeepSDF. It modifies the computation of grid edge points and grid cell points to avoid relying on distance information, which is often unavailable or unreliable in occupancy fields. By introducing auxiliary 2D points to compute local surface normals, ODC identifies 3D points via the Quadric Error Function (QEF) and utilizes fast, parallelizable search algorithms for 1D, 2D, and 3D points.
-
-### [Mass Point Centering for Dual Contouring](3939-mass-point-centering-for-dual-contouring.md)
-Mass Point Centering is a numerical stabilization technique for solving the Quadratic Error Function (QEF) in Dual Contouring. It addresses the ill-conditioning that arises when the local surface geometry is far from the coordinate origin by shifting the linear system to be centered around the centroid of the sample points. Unlike [Brute-Force QEF Solver](4403-brute-force-qef-solver.md) which may suffer from precision loss with large coordinates, this method ensures the solution space is well-conditioned without altering the geometric accuracy of the resulting dual vertex.
+A method designed to extract meshes from neural implicit functions (e.g., Occupancy Networks) where distance information is unavailable. It modifies grid edge/cell point computation to avoid relying on distance, using auxiliary 2D points for normals and fast parallelizable searches for 1D/2D/3D points. Choose this when working with occupancy fields rather than SDFs. Link: [Occupancy-Based Dual Contouring](3799-occupancy-based-dual-contouring.md).
+
+### Mass Point Centering
+A numerical stabilization technique that shifts the linear system to be centered around the centroid of sample points, addressing ill-conditioning from large coordinates [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Brute-Force QEF Solver](4403-brute-force-qef-solver.md), it ensures a well-conditioned solution space without altering geometric accuracy. Choose this when vertex coordinates are far from the origin. Link: [Mass Point Centering for Dual Contouring](3939-mass-point-centering-for-dual-contouring.md).
 
 ### [Brute-Force QEF Solver](4403-brute-force-qef-solver.md)
-The Brute-Force QEF Solver is a method for solving the Quadratic Error Function (QEF) by performing a grid search over the unit cube $[0,1]^3$ to find the point with the minimum squared distance to a set of planes, rather than using a closed-form linear algebra solution. It serves as a fallback or simple implementation for the Dual Contouring QEF Solver [Parent](1424-dual-contouring-qef-solver.md). It is distinct from [Quadric Error Function (QEF) for Sharp Features](1604-quadric-error-function-qef-for-sharp-features.md) which handles non-smooth features, and [QEF Data Accumulation](3712-qef-data-accumulation.md) which gathers the plane data. It is chosen when computational resources are sufficient for a coarse search or when a robust, dependency-free solver is required, avoiding the numerical instability of matrix inversion.
+A method that performs a grid search over the unit cube $[0,1]^3$ to find the point with minimum squared distance to plane constraints, rather than using closed-form linear algebra. It preserves sharp features but is inefficient. Note: In 3D DC implementations, this is often disabled in favor of analytical solvers. Link: [Brute-Force QEF Solver](4403-brute-force-qef-solver.md).
 
 ### [QEF Solver Initialization](4722-qef-solver-initialization.md)
-QEF Solver Initialization prepares the Quadratic Error Function (QEF) system for numerical solution by computing the centroid of the input data and assembling the symmetric linear system matrices $A^T A$ and $A^T b$. It serves as the prerequisite step for the [Dual Contouring QEF Solver](1424-dual-contouring-qef-solver.md), distinguishing itself from [QEF Data Accumulation](3712-qef-data-accumulation.md) by transitioning from raw data collection to the construction of the solvable linear algebraic structure.
+Prepares the QEF system by computing the centroid of accumulated data and assembling the symmetric linear system matrices $A^T A$ and $A^T b$. It transitions from raw data collection to the solvable linear algebraic structure. Choose this as the first step before invoking the main solver. Link: [QEF Solver Initialization](4722-qef-solver-initialization.md).
 
 ### Newton Surface Projection
-Newton Surface Projection is a post-processing step that projects all extracted vertices exactly onto the isosurface using Newton's method, achieving machine precision accuracy [S3, unofficial]. After Dual Contouring vertex placement, it applies Newton iteration to each vertex position to minimize the distance to the isosurface defined by the implicit function [S3, unofficial].
+A post-processing step that projects all extracted vertices exactly onto the isosurface using Newton's method, achieving machine precision accuracy. It is applied after Dual Contouring vertex placement to minimize distance to the implicit function. Choose this when exact surface adherence is required beyond the QEF approximation. Link: [Newton Surface Projection](1604-quadric-error-function-qef-for-sharp-features.md) (Note: No dedicated article, described in).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: The core QEF solve is $O(1)$ per voxel for a $3 \times 3$ system, dominated by the SVD/eigensolver which is constant time for fixed dimension [G]. The overall complexity is linear with respect to the number of voxels $V$, $O(V)$ [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(V)$ for storing voxel data and normals [G]. The solver itself uses constant memory per voxel [G].
-*   **Performance**: On an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090, the implementation achieves >230 M faces/s on sparse Fandisk CAD at $1024^3$ resolution (14.36 ms latency for 3,432,768 triangles) [S1, unofficial]. For pure quad output, throughput is 134 M quads/s [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths**: Preserves sharp features (creases, apexes) better than Dual Marching Cubes by placing vertices directly on sharp creases via QEF SVD [S1, unofficial]. Produces 100% watertight manifold topology ($\chi=2$) using Deterministic Cartesian Quadrant Slotting [S1, unofficial]. Fully differentiable, allowing backpropagation of gradients into SDF values and colors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations**: Single dual vertex per cell limitation can cause non-manifold geometry in complex sub-voxel topologies [S1, unofficial]. Requires boundary padding (`pad=1`) for sparse grids [S1, unofficial]. Performance depends on the quality of normals; trilinear gradients can blur sharp edges [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use**: High-quality surface extraction from SDFs or neural implicit fields where sharp feature preservation and watertight topology are critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use**: When sub-voxel topology is extremely complex (multiple sheets per voxel) and vertex sharing is not acceptable, or when computational resources are too limited for SVD operations (though $3 \times 3$ SVD is very fast) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of sign-crossing edges per voxel and the cost of the $3 \times 3$ SVD. The SVD is constant time per voxel. The lookup table approach is $O(1)$ for topology generation.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for vertices and faces, where $V$ is the number of voxels. Sparse grids reduce this significantly.
+*   **Measured Performance:** On an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090, Dual Contouring achieves >230 M faces/s on sparse Fandisk CAD at $1024^3$ resolution (14.36 ms latency for 3,432,768 triangles) [S1, unofficial]. Pure quad output achieves 134 M quads/s [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Preserves sharp features (creases, apexes) better than Dual Marching Cubes by placing vertices directly on sharp creases via QEF SVD [S1, unofficial]. Produces 100% watertight manifold topology ($\chi=2$) using deterministic Cartesian quadrant slotting [S1, unofficial]. Fully differentiable, allowing backpropagation into SDF values and colors [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Single dual vertex per cell limits representation of complex sub-voxel topology [S1, unofficial]. Requires exact normals to avoid blurring [S1, unofficial]. Sparse grids require boundary padding [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When sharp feature preservation and watertight topology are critical, and exact normals are available.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When the input geometry has multiple disconnected surfaces within a single voxel, or when only approximate normals (trilinear gradients) are available and sharp features are not required.
 
 ## Background
-Dual Contouring was introduced by Ju et al. in 2002 as a method to extract watertight meshes from volumetric data by placing a single vertex in each intersecting voxel [S1, unofficial]. The QEF solver variant described here is a modern, high-performance CUDA implementation (e.g., `conquer3d`) that enhances the original method with regularized SVD for numerical stability and deterministic topology generation [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitations of earlier dual contouring implementations that suffered from self-intersecting quads and poor sharp feature preservation [S1, unofficial]. The method is widely used in real-time rendering and CAD visualization where exact manifold topology and sharp features are required [S1, unofficial].
+Dual Contouring was introduced by Ju et al. in 2002 as an improvement over Marching Cubes for preserving sharp features [S1, unofficial]. The method places a single vertex in each voxel containing an isosurface intersection, determined by minimizing a Quadratic Error Function (QEF) based on surface normals. Modern implementations, such as those in `conquer3d`, utilize Jacobi SVD solvers and deterministic lookup tables to ensure robustness and performance on GPUs [S1, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in CAD modeling and neural implicit surface reconstruction where sharp features and manifold topology are essential.
 
